Consider the two-dimensional fixed-frequency scalar diffraction problem for two slits. Let Γ ⊂ R denote the union of two disjoint bounded open intervals (the "slits"), placed in the screen R × {0} ⊂ R^2, and let the screen S = (R × {0}) \ (Γ × {0}) be a perfectly reflecting (e.g. Dirichlet or Neumann) obstacle. Given a wave number k > 0 and an incoming time-harmonic plane wave u^{inc}(x,y) = e^{i k (x sin θ + y cos θ)} incident from the upper half-plane, find the total field u(x,y) = u^{inc} + u^{sc} such that:
(i) u satisfies the Helmholtz equation (Δ + k^2) u = 0 in R^2 \ (S × {0});
(ii) u satisfies the prescribed boundary condition (u = 0 or ∂_n u = 0) on the screen S;
(iii) u^{sc} satisfies the Sommerfeld radiation condition at infinity.

Provide a rigorous and explicit closed-form mathematical representation of the solution u (for instance, in terms of known special functions and explicitly computable integrals), together with explicit asymptotics of u^{sc} in the far field as |(x,y)| → ∞, valid for arbitrary positions and widths of the two slits and arbitrary k > 0 and incidence angle θ.

